[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Run commands recorded in Phase A:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | program | command |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | --- | --- |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | C | `c_src/build/driver` (reads stdin, writes stdout/stderr) |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Rust | `translation/target/release/driver` (same) |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatches found
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 1. `compact_runs` with threshold 1 overflows the C program's stack buffer
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Status: unresolved by design — the C behaviour here is undefined, not a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m behaviour that can be replicated.**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `main.c` declares `uint8_t buffer[256]` and `process_buffer` is free to grow the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m logical length. When flag `0x02` is set and `param1 == 1`, `compact_runs`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m rewrites *every* run — including runs of length 1 — as the two-byte pair
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `value, count`. Each single-element run therefore adds one byte to `len`, and a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m buffer of `N` distinct bytes ends up `2 * N` long. For `N > 128` the C program
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m writes past `buffer[255]`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Observed on this machine (`gcc 11.5.0`, x86-64):
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | input | C stdout | C status | Rust stdout | Rust status |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `2 1 0 128` + 128 distinct bytes | `256 0 1 1 ...` | 0 | identical | 0 |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `2 1 0 140` + 140 distinct bytes | `280 0 1 1 ...` | 0 | identical | 0 |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `2 1 0 160` + 160 distinct bytes | *(empty)* | killed by SIGSEGV | `320 0 1 1 ...` | 0 |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `2 1 0 256` + 256 distinct bytes | *(empty)* | killed by SIGSEGV | `512 0 1 1 ...` | 0 |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stdout is empty in the crashing cases because stdout is a fully buffered pipe
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and the process dies before the flush at `return 0`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** Out-of-bounds stack writes clobber the saved return address of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `main`. Whether that is fatal depends purely on the stack frame layout the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m compiler chose, so the boundary is not a property of the source:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | C build | last non-crashing result length | first crashing result length |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | --- | --- | --- |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | no `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE` (`-O0`) | 312 | 314 |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug` (`-O0 -g`) | 312 | 314 |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release` (`-O3`) | 280 | 282 |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Because the same C source produces two different boundaries from two different
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m compiler invocations, there is no boundary to translate. The Rust program
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m instead over-allocates the buffer (`BUFFER_CAPACITY = 1024`, zero filled) and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m keeps running, which reproduces the C output exactly for every input where the C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m program stays inside its own array — and, incidentally, for the padding region
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m up to length 312 on the `-O0` build.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Test policy.** `tests/common/mod.rs::c_overflows` identifies this class
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (`flags & 0x02 != 0 && threshold == 1 && length > 128`) and the differential
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m tests skip it. Nothing is `#[ignore]`d: the excluded inputs are excluded from
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the *generated input set*, not from assertion. The growth path itself is still
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m covered up to and including `length == 128`, where the final length is exactly
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 256 and the C program is still in bounds
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (`compact_runs_growth_path`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Threshold 1 is the only way to reach this: `threshold` is `param1` when
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `0 < param1 <= 255` and otherwise 3, so `param1 == 1` is required, and a run of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m length 2 or more never grows.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Behaviours checked and found already faithful
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m No other mismatch was found. The following are the traps a translation of this
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m program can plausibly get wrong; each was confirmed identical by running both
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m binaries, and each has a dedicated test.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`rotate_buffer` rotates in two different directions.** The
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `offset < len / 2` branch moves the prefix aside and shifts the remainder
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   down, which is a *left* rotation; the `else` branch is a *right* rotation.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `1 2 0 10 0 1 ... 9` gives `2 3 4 5 6 7 8 9 0 1` while `1 7 0 10 ...` gives
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `3 4 5 6 7 8 9 0 1 2`. Covered by `rotate_all_offsets` / `rotate_extremes`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`rotate_buffer`'s chunk loop always runs once.** `chunk` is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `min(offset, 256)` and `offset < len <= 256`, so `chunk == offset` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `i += chunk` exits the loop after the first iteration.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`compact_runs` caps a run at 255 but still advances `read` by the capped
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   value.** 256 identical bytes with any threshold `<= 255` yields
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `3 7 255 7`, not `2 7 255`: the 256th byte survives as a leftover run.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   Covered by `compact_runs_caps_at_255`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`interleave_halves` reads bytes it has already overwritten.** The loop
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   writes `buf[i*2+1] = buf[half+i]` in place, so once `i*2+1 >= half` it is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   reading its own output. The odd-length fixup `buf[len-1] = buf[half]` reads an
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   already-clobbered slot too. `8 0 0 7 1 2 3 4 5 6 7` yields
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `1 4 2 5 3 6 5` — note the duplicated `5` and the lost `7`. Covered by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `interleave_length_guard_and_parities`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`interleave_halves`' `half > 256` branch is unreachable.** It needs
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `new_len >= 514`, beyond anything `process_buffer` can produce from a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   256-byte input. Kept in the translation for fidelity; no test can reach it.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Order of the flag stages, and the length guards between them.** `0x08`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   requires `new_len >= 2` and `0x10` requires `new_len >= 4`, both evaluated
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   against the length *after* compaction and de-duplication, and `0x10`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   additionally needs `seg_size <= new_len`. Covered by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `every_flag_combination_on_representative_buffers`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`param1` is overloaded three ways** — rotation offset, run threshold and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   segment size — so a single value drives all three stages when several flags
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   are set. Covered by the same test.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`scanf` ignores line structure.** `3\n1\n0\n4\n1\n2\n3\n4\n` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `3 1 0 4 1 2 3 4` are equivalent, and a missing trailing newline changes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   nothing. Covered by `whitespace_and_stream_shape`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **glibc `%u` / `%zu` accept a minus sign and wrap.** `0 0 0 -1` is read as
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `length == 18446744073709551615` and reported as
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `Error: length 18446744073709551615 exceeds maximum 256`, exit 1 — not as a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   read failure. Covered by `length_exceeds_maximum`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Out-of-range conversions clamp before they narrow.** `%u` goes through
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `strtoul` and saturates at `ULONG_MAX` before truncation to `unsigned int`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   so `99999999999999999999999999` for `flags` becomes `0xFFFFFFFF`; `%d` goes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   through `strtol`, saturates at `LONG_MAX`/`LONG_MIN`, and then narrows to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `int`, so an absurdly large `param1` becomes `-1`. Covered by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `integer_conversion_edges`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Partial numeric parses.** `0x10` is read as `0` (base 10 stops at `x`) and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the leftover `x10` fails the *next* conversion, so the reported error is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `Error reading param1`, not `Error reading flags`. Likewise `1.5` yields `1`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   and then fails on `.`. Covered by `error_reading_param1` /
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `error_reading_param2`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Error precedence.** The length bound is checked before any buffer byte is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   read, so `0 0 0 300 1 2 3` reports the length error and never reports a byte
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   error. Each of the six `fprintf(stderr, ...)` sites, its exact wording and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   its exit code 1 has a test.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    138[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`length == 0` short-circuits `process_buffer` before the flag dispatch**,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    139[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   printing `0\n` for every flag/parameter combination. Covered by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `zero_length_returns_zero_for_every_flag_combination`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    141[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Output formatting.** `printf("%zu", n)` then `printf(" %u", byte)` per
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    142[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   element then a single `"\n"`, which means length `0` prints `0\n` with no
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    143[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   trailing space. Asserted byte for byte everywhere.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    144[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    145[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Coverage evidence
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    146[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    147[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Beyond the 23 committed tests, the two programs were compared over
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    148[0m:[38;5;10m[49m approximately 600,000 additional inputs while investigating:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    149[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    150[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - 432,128 inputs: flags `0..32` × 32 `param1` values × 2 `param2` values × 96
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    151[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   buffer patterns (all binary patterns up to length 6, plus 3-symbol and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    152[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   longer patterns) — 0 mismatches.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    153[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - 140,768 inputs: 8 structured pattern families (all-identical, all-distinct,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    154[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   alternating, block runs, 255-long runs, single-run-plus-tail) at lengths
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    155[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   1–4 and 127–256 × 23 `param1` values × flags `0..32` — 0 mismatches.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    156[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - ~30,000 random inputs plus 79 hand-written malformed-input cases — 0
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    157[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   mismatches outside the overflow class above.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    158[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    159[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The harness itself was mutation-tested: reverting the `interleave_halves`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    160[0m:[38;5;10m[49m clobbering read, changing the `compact_runs` cap from 255 to 254, and changing
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    161[0m:[38;5;10m[49m one `exit(1)` to `exit(0)` each caused a test failure, confirming the suite
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    162[0m:[38;5;10m[49m checks stdout, stderr and exit status rather than passing vacuously.
